        "title": "Cells: The Building Blocks of Life",
        "reading": "Every living thing on Earth is built from cells. You are built from about thirty trillion of them. A tree, a mushroom, and a bacterium are all made of cells too. The cell is the smallest unit of life: anything smaller is not alive on its own. In the 1800s, scientists put these facts together into the cell theory. It says that all living things are made of cells, that cells are the basic unit of life, and that all cells come from other cells.\n\nThink of cells as tiny rooms where life happens. Each cell is wrapped in a thin skin called the cell membrane, which controls what enters and leaves. Inside is a jelly-like fluid called cytoplasm, where much of the cell's work gets done. Most cells also hold a nucleus, a round control center that stores instructions for the cell. These parts work together the way the rooms of a house serve one family.\n\nCells do not all look or act the same. A skin cell is flat and tough, a muscle cell is long and stretchy, and a nerve cell has branches that carry messages. Yet each one is still a cell, following the same basic plan. When you understand the cell, you hold the key to every living thing, from the bacteria in yogurt to the cells in your brain.",
