        "title": "Seeing the Invisible World",
        "reading": "Cells are far too small to see with your eyes alone. Most are measured in micrometers, which are millionths of a meter. Hundreds of cells could fit across the period at the end of this sentence. For thousands of years, no one knew cells existed. Then, in the 1660s, an English scientist named Robert Hooke looked at thin cork through a microscope he built himself. The tiny boxlike rooms reminded him of the small rooms where monks lived, called cells, and the name stuck.\n\nBetter microscopes kept revealing smaller wonders. Scientists saw single-celled creatures swimming in pond water and watched blood cells race through vessels. They learned that cells are not empty boxes but busy workshops. Each stronger lens opened a new world. Today, powerful microscopes can show the parts inside a cell in sharp detail, and scientists can even watch living cells divide in real time.\n\nSeeing cells changed medicine forever. Once doctors knew that diseases could attack cells, they could study exactly where the damage happened. When scientists learned that the body constantly makes new cells, they understood how cuts heal and bones mend. The invisible world turned out to run the visible one, and the microscope became one of the most important tools in science.",
