        "reading": "Plant cells do something animal cells cannot: they make their own food from sunlight. The secret sits in small green parts called chloroplasts, which trap light energy and turn it into sugar. That process is photosynthesis, and it feeds nearly every living thing on Earth. Without plant cells, there would be no crops, no forests, and no oxygen to breathe.\n\nPlant cells carry special parts for their tough, sunlit life. A stiff cell wall surrounds the membrane and holds the plant upright, like the frame of a house. A large central vacuole stores water and keeps the cell firm. When the vacuole loses water, the plant wilts. Chloroplasts float in the cytoplasm, catching sunlight all day long.\n\nThese parts explain things you see every day. Leaves are green because chloroplasts are packed with a green pigment called chlorophyll. A wilted plant perks up after watering because its vacuoles refill with water. The next time you eat a salad, remember that you are eating millions of tiny solar panels, each one turning light into lunch.\n\nThe next time you see a houseplant leaning toward a window, you are watching plant cells at work. Each leaf turns to catch more light for its chloroplasts. The wall holds it up, the vacuole keeps it firm, and the green pigment does the rest. A plant is a quiet factory, and sunlight is its raw material.",
