        "title": "Pharaohs of the Nile",
        "reading": "Around 3100 BCE, a king named Narmer united the villages along the Nile into one kingdom: Egypt. The Egyptians believed their king was a living god. They called him pharaoh, which meant great house, and they believed he owned all the land. When the Nile flooded each year, it left rich black soil, and the pharaoh's officials measured the fields and collected taxes in grain.\n\nThe pharaoh's power stretched far beyond Egypt's borders, and far up the Nile lay the African kingdom of Nubia. For centuries Egypt and Nubia traded and fought, but around 750 BCE the tables turned. Nubian kings marched north and took the Egyptian throne. These rulers, called the 25th Dynasty, were Black pharaohs from Nubia who ruled all of Egypt for nearly a hundred years and built pyramids of their own.\n\nOne Nubian pharaoh, Piye, left an inscription telling his story: \"I am Piye, king of Nubia. The gods of Egypt have given me this land. I did not burn a single city that opened its gates to me. I shall rule with justice and honor the temples.\" His words show a conqueror who wanted to be seen as a rightful Egyptian king, not a foreign invader. Egypt's story was never one people alone. It was African from its first stone to its last.",
