        "title": "A Land of City-States",
        "reading": "Greece was not one country but many small ones. Rocky mountains and deep seas split the land into valleys, and each valley built its own city-state, called a polis. Athens, Sparta, Corinth, and Thebes each had their own government, army, coins, and gods. The people of one polis often saw their neighbors as foreigners, even though they all spoke Greek.\n\nThe two most famous poleis could not have been more different. Athens prized debate, art, and new ideas. Sparta prized discipline, war, and obedience. An Athenian boy learned reading and music, while a Spartan boy was taken from home at age seven to train as a soldier. Yet both cities told stories, and one of Greece's most beloved storytellers was Aesop, an African man enslaved in Greece whose fables about clever animals are still told today.\n\nAthens tried something no city had tried before. Its leader Cleisthenes gave ordinary citizens a share of power, letting them vote in an assembly and serve on juries. It was not fair by our standards: women, enslaved people, and foreigners could not vote. But the idea was planted: government could belong to the people. Pericles, Athens's great leader, boasted: \"Our government favors the many, not the few. We call it rule by the people, and every citizen is equal before the law.\"",
