        "title": "Mendel's Pea Plants",
        "reading": "In the 1860s, a monk named Gregor Mendel grew pea plants in a monastery garden and changed science forever. He chose peas because they grow fast and show clear traits: tall or short stems, green or yellow seeds, smooth or wrinkled skins. By crossing plants and counting thousands of offspring, Mendel found the hidden rules of inheritance.\n\nMendel's biggest discovery was the 3 to 1 ratio. When he crossed a tall plant with a short plant, all the children were tall. But when those children were crossed, about three quarters of the grandchildren were tall and one quarter were short. The short trait had been hiding, waiting for its chance to appear.\n\nMendel called the hiding traits recessive and the visible ones dominant. He guessed that each plant carries two copies of instructions for each trait, one from each parent. Nobody listened at first. His paper sat unread for thirty-five years, until scientists rediscovered it in 1900 and realized he had found the laws of genetics.\n\nMendel's garden is gone, but his method remains. Every genetics lab still crosses, counts, and looks for ratios. The monk who counted peas taught science its most powerful habit: when the numbers form a pattern, nature is telling you something.",
