        "title": "Democracy Is Born",
        "reading": "Around 508 BCE, an Athenian leader named Cleisthenes broke the power of the nobles. He divided Athens into new voting tribes that mixed rich and poor neighborhoods, so no noble family could control the vote. Then he opened the assembly to all citizens. Any free adult male born in Athens could now stand on the hill, speak his mind, and cast his vote on war, peace, and law.\n\nAthens also invented a strange safety valve called ostracism. Once a year, citizens could vote to banish any person for ten years by scratching a name on a piece of broken pottery. It was meant to stop any one man from becoming a tyrant. The system worked, sometimes too well: the great general Themistocles, who had saved Greece from Persia, was ostracized by the city he rescued.\n\nThe Greek love of liberty echoed across the centuries. In the 1800s, the formerly enslaved American writer Frederick Douglass used Greece's own ideals against slavery. He argued: \"The Greeks taught the world that all men love liberty. America praises these ideals while holding millions in chains. If liberty is right for Athens, it is right for every person.\" Douglass turned ancient words into a weapon for freedom.",
