        "title": "Cracks in the Empire",
        "reading": "By the 200s CE, Rome was too big to govern. The empire stretched from Britain to Egypt, and messages took months to cross it. Emperors rose and fell in bloody turns: in one fifty-year span, more than twenty men claimed the throne. Taxes climbed, coins lost value, and farmers abandoned their fields.\n\nWorse, the army that had built the empire now strained to defend it. Germanic peoples the Romans called barbarians pressed against the borders, some fleeing fiercer tribes behind them. Rome hired many of them as soldiers, and soon the legions were half barbarian. In 410 CE, the unthinkable happened: the Visigoths sacked Rome itself.\n\nWhile Rome cracked, another African kingdom shone. The Kingdom of Aksum, in what is now Ethiopia, thrived as Rome fell. Aksum minted its own coins, raised towering stone obelisks, and traded with India and Arabia. It adopted Christianity and built a Christian civilization that still stands today. As one empire dimmed, another African light burned bright.\n\nThe fall was slow enough that many Romans never noticed it happening. They still paid taxes, still cheered races, still called themselves Roman long after the emperors were gone. Decline is quiet. That is what makes it dangerous. Empires rarely hear themselves fall.",
