        "title": "Clues in Our Bodies",
        "reading": "Evolution left evidence inside you. The human arm, the bat's wing, the whale's flipper, and the cat's paw all share the same bone pattern: one bone, two bones, many small bones, digits. Different jobs, same blueprint. These are called homologous structures, and they point to a shared ancestor.\n\nDNA gives the final proof. Humans and chimpanzees share about 98 percent of their DNA. The more closely two species are related, the more DNA they share. Scientists can even find broken genes, old instructions switched off long ago, sitting in our DNA like dusty books on a shelf.\n\nRocks, bodies, and DNA tell the same story three ways. Fossils show change over time. Homologous structures show shared blueprints. DNA shows shared code. Three separate lines of evidence, one conclusion: life on Earth evolved.\n\nYour own body carries one more clue: goosebumps. When you are cold or scared, tiny muscles pull your hairs upright, just as they raise an animal's fur for warmth or warning. On your nearly hairless skin, the reflex does nothing useful. It is a leftover instruction from a furrier past, still running in you today. Your tailbone tells the very same story: a leftover from ancestors who had tails.",
