        "title": "Sorting All Life",
        "reading": "Scientists have named about 1.8 million species, and millions more wait to be found. To keep order, biologists sort all life into groups, a science called classification. The groups nest like boxes: each species belongs to a genus, each genus to a family, and so on up to kingdom and domain.\n\nThe system began with Carl Linnaeus in the 1700s. He gave every species a two-part Latin name, like Homo sapiens for humans. The first word names the genus, the second names the species. One name, used worldwide, so scientists never confuse a puma, a cougar, and a mountain lion again.\n\nModern classification also shows relationships. Lions and tigers share the genus Panthera, so they are close cousins. Humans share the order Primates with monkeys and apes. The boxes do more than organize. They draw the tree of life.\n\nTry sorting your own world. List five animals you know and group them by shared traits: fur, feathers, scales, or none. You have just built a tiny classification. Linnaeus did the same for all life, one careful box at a time. Start with your pets, then try the birds outside your window. Soon you will see why Linnaeus needed boxes within boxes.",
