        "title": "Bacteria: Friend and Foe",
        "reading": "Bacteria are single-celled microbes, far smaller than your cells. A spoonful of soil holds more bacteria than there are people on Earth. Most are harmless, and many are helpful. The bacteria in yogurt turn milk thick and tangy. The trillions in your gut help digest food and train your immune system.\n\nA few bacteria cause disease. They are not evil; they are simply living the only way they know, feeding and multiplying. Cholera, tuberculosis, and strep throat all come from harmful bacteria. Before germ theory, no one knew these tiny creatures caused so much suffering.\n\nHelpful or harmful, bacteria follow the same plan: a single cell with no nucleus, copying itself by splitting in two. Some double every twenty minutes. That speed is their superpower, and it is why a small infection can become serious fast, and why helpful bacteria can culture a whole jar of yogurt overnight.\n\nNot all bacteria are germs, and not all germs are bacteria. Viruses are even smaller and work differently, which is why antibiotics cannot touch them. Knowing your enemy matters: the right weapon depends on the right identification. That is why good doctors always ask what is making you sick before they prescribe anything.",
