        "title": "Feudalism: A Chain of Promises",
        "reading": "After the Roman Empire fell, western Europe broke into small kingdoms with weak kings. A king could not protect every village, so a new system grew up called feudalism. In feudalism, the king granted land called a fief to a powerful nobleman. That nobleman promised loyalty and military help in return, and he became the king's vassal. The vassal could then divide his own land and grant pieces to his own vassals, creating a chain of promises from the king down to the local lord.\n\nEvery link in the chain had duties and rights. A vassal owed his lord forty days of military service each year, advice at the lord's court, and money payments on special occasions. In return, the lord owed the vassal protection, justice, and the land itself. These promises were sealed in a solemn ceremony called homage, where the vassal knelt and swore to be faithful.\n\nThe system was orderly but unfair: lords and kings held the power while peasants did almost all the work. Far away in West Africa, a different kind of bond held society together. The griots of Mali were storytellers and historians who preserved the history of kings and heroes, much like the bards of medieval Europe. Their songs carried laws, family lines, and the deeds of great rulers from one generation to the next. Feudalism kept Europe together for centuries, yet it also froze society in place. A peasant's children were almost always peasants, and a lord's children were almost always lords.",
