        "title": "The Heart: Your Hardest-Working Muscle",
        "reading": "Your heart is about the size of your fist, and it never takes a day off. This powerful muscle pumps blood through about 60,000 miles of blood vessels, enough to circle the Earth more than twice. With every beat, the heart pushes oxygen-rich blood out to the body and pulls oxygen-poor blood back to the lungs for a refill. It does this about 100,000 times every single day.\n\nBlood has three main jobs. Red blood cells carry oxygen. White blood cells fight germs. Platelets help the blood clot when you get a cut. Plasma, the liquid part of blood, carries nutrients, hormones, and waste. Together they keep every cell in your body supplied and clean.\n\nYour heart rate is the number of beats per minute, and it changes with activity. At rest, a typical heart beats 60 to 100 times per minute. During exercise, it can double or triple to deliver extra oxygen to working muscles. Athletes often have lower resting heart rates because their hearts are strong and efficient. Doctors measure heart rate because it is a quick window into how hard the heart is working. Like a castle's guards watching the gates, your heart stands watch over your whole body, beat after beat, for your entire life.",
