        "title": "Why Did They Go? Motives for the Crusades",
        "reading": "In 1095, Pope Urban II called on Christians to march to the Holy Land and free Jerusalem from Muslim rule. His speech launched the Crusades, a series of religious wars that lasted nearly two hundred years. Thousands answered the call. Knights hoped for adventure, land, and forgiveness of sins. Younger sons who would inherit nothing saw a chance to win estates. Merchants saw new trade routes, and ordinary pilgrims joined out of deep faith.\n\nThe crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099 and set up small Christian kingdoms along the coast. Over the next centuries, control of the Holy Land passed back and forth between Christians and Muslims. Famous leaders rose on both sides, including Richard the Lionheart of England and Saladin, the Muslim sultan admired even by his enemies for his honor.\n\nThe Crusades also changed trade. As Europeans traveled east, they met goods, ideas, and knowledge from a wider world. Gold from African kingdoms like Mali flowed across the Sahara to Mediterranean markets, and some of that wealth traveled the same eastern routes the crusaders used. The Crusades failed to hold the Holy Land, but they cracked open Europe's isolation and connected it to Africa and Asia in lasting ways. Motives were mixed: faith, greed, adventure, and curiosity traveled in the same army.",
