        "title": "Genghis Khan and the Mongol Empire",
        "reading": "In the early 1200s, a Mongol leader named Temujin united the warring tribes of the Asian steppe and took the title Genghis Khan, meaning universal ruler. His armies were fast, disciplined, and terrifying. Mongol horsemen could ride for days, shoot arrows accurately at full gallop, and coordinate attacks with signal flags across miles of battlefield. Within his lifetime, Genghis conquered more land than any leader in history, from China to Persia.\n\nHis grandsons pushed even further. By 1279, the Mongol Empire stretched from Korea to Hungary, the largest land empire the world has ever seen. It was divided among his descendants into khanates, each ruled by a khan. The empire was brutal in conquest: cities that resisted were destroyed as warnings to others. But cities that surrendered were often spared and folded into a vast system of trade and taxation.\n\nThe Mongols' wealth and power invite comparison with another great empire of the age. In West Africa, Mansa Musa of Mali ruled a kingdom so rich in gold that his fortune rivaled the Mongol khans. When Musa made his famous pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, he gave away so much gold that prices fell in every city he visited. Two empires, one built on horses and conquest and one on gold and trade, each showed what organized power could achieve in the 1300s.",
