        "title": "The Renaissance: A Rebirth of Ideas",
        "reading": "After the Middle Ages, Europe experienced a burst of creativity called the Renaissance, which means rebirth. It began in Italy's wealthy trading cities around the 1300s. Rich merchant families paid artists and thinkers to create beautiful works, and scholars rediscovered the writings of ancient Greece and Rome. This new focus on human potential and learning was called humanism. Instead of only thinking about heaven, people began celebrating what humans could achieve in this life.\n\nRenaissance art changed how the world looked on canvas. Painters learned perspective, a technique that makes flat paintings seem three-dimensional. Sculptors carved lifelike bodies. Writers like Shakespeare explored human nature in plays people still perform. Scientists began testing ideas with experiments instead of trusting old authorities. The printing press, invented around 1440, spread these new ideas across Europe faster than ever before.\n\nThe Renaissance was not only a European story. Trade and ideas flowed through many cultures, and Africans were part of Renaissance Europe. Alessandro de' Medici, who became Duke of Florence in 1530, was of African descent through his mother, and he ruled one of Italy's most famous cities during the Renaissance itself. His story reminds us that Black people were present at the heart of this age, not just watching from the edges. The Renaissance was a rebirth, and its light reached further than many textbooks admit.",
