        "title": "Earthquakes: When the Ground Moves",
        "reading": "An earthquake is the shaking of Earth's surface caused by the sudden release of energy underground. Most earthquakes happen at tectonic plate boundaries, where stress builds as plates push, pull, or grind against each other. Rocks bend and store energy like a stretched rubber band. When the stress finally overcomes the friction holding the rocks together, they snap to a new position, and the released energy races outward as seismic waves.\n\nThe point underground where the break begins is called the focus. The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter. Scientists locate epicenters by comparing when seismic waves arrive at different monitoring stations. The closer a station is, the sooner the waves arrive.\n\nEarthquakes are measured on the moment magnitude scale, which replaced the older Richter scale. Each whole-number step represents about 32 times more energy released. A magnitude 6 quake releases roughly 32 times the energy of a magnitude 5. Most earthquakes are too small to feel, but the largest ones can topple cities. Because earthquakes strike without warning, scientists focus on preparation: strong buildings, emergency plans, and early-warning systems that detect fast waves before the damaging ones arrive. Like sailors watching the sea, scientists watch the Earth for signs of what comes next.",
