        "title": "The Columbian Exchange: Winners and Losers",
        "reading": "After 1492, the Americas and the rest of the world began swapping plants, animals, diseases, and ideas. Historians call this the Columbian Exchange. Europe gained potatoes, tomatoes, corn, and chocolate, crops that transformed diets and fed growing populations. The Americas received wheat, horses, cattle, and pigs, which reshaped farming and daily life. Sugar cane, brought to the Caribbean, became enormously profitable and tragically drove the demand for enslaved labor.\n\nBut the exchange had a devastating side. Europeans carried diseases like smallpox and measles to which Native peoples had no immunity. Epidemic after epidemic swept through communities, and millions died. This was not a fair trade. One side gained new foods while the other faced catastrophe.\n\nAmid this upheaval, enslaved Africans brought knowledge that transformed American farming. They carried rice-growing skills from West Africa and made Carolina's rice plantations productive. They planted okra, black-eyed peas, and other African crops that became staples of Southern cooking. The Columbian Exchange is often told as a European story, but African hands and African knowledge shaped what grew in American soil. The exchange also moved people against their will, and their suffering is part of the story. Winners and losers depended on which side of the ocean you stood.",
