        "title": "Weathering: Breaking Rock Down",
        "reading": "Even the mightiest mountain cannot resist weathering, the slow breaking down of rock at Earth's surface. Weathering comes in two forms. Mechanical weathering breaks rock into smaller pieces without changing what it is made of. Water seeps into cracks, freezes, and expands, prying rocks apart like a wedge. Plant roots grow into cracks and split boulders. Wind-blown sand sandblasts exposed stone.\n\nChemical weathering changes the minerals themselves. Rainwater, slightly acidic, dissolves limestone and carves caves. Oxygen rusts iron-rich rocks, turning them reddish brown. Living things add their own chemistry: lichens secrete acids that etch stone. Together, mechanical and chemical weathering turn solid bedrock into loose fragments.\n\nWeathering works fastest where conditions are extreme. In deserts, daily heating and cooling crack rocks apart. In wet tropics, heavy rain and warmth speed chemical breakdown. Weathering is the first step in a long journey: broken rock becomes sediment, sediment becomes soil, and soil grows the food that feeds the world. Without weathering, there would be no soil, no farms, and no forests. Destruction, in nature, is also creation. Weathering never works alone. Once rock breaks apart, erosion carries the pieces away by wind, water, or ice. Together, weathering and erosion sculpt canyons, smooth pebbles, and build the beaches we enjoy. The process never stops, not even for a single day.",
