        "title": "The Enlightenment: The Power of Reason",
        "reading": "In the 1600s and 1700s, European thinkers launched a movement called the Enlightenment. They argued that reason, clear thinking based on facts and logic, should guide human life instead of tradition or unquestioned authority. Scientists like Isaac Newton showed that the universe followed discoverable laws. Philosophers asked bold questions: What makes a government legitimate? Do all people have natural rights? Their answers would soon shake kingdoms.\n\nEnlightenment ideas spread through books, newspapers, and lively debates in coffeehouses and salons. Thinkers argued that all people are born with rights to life, liberty, and property, and that governments exist to protect those rights. If a government failed, the people could change it. These were radical claims in a world ruled by kings who claimed God gave them power.\n\nThe claim that all people have rights faced an obvious challenge: slavery and inequality surrounded these thinkers. Yet Black minds proved the point the philosophers only debated. Phillis Wheatley, an enslaved woman in Boston, published poetry so brilliant that skeptics demanded proof she wrote it. Benjamin Banneker, a free Black mathematician and astronomer, helped survey the new capital city and challenged Thomas Jefferson himself on slavery. Their achievements showed that intellect knows no color, and they turned Enlightenment ideals into a demand no one could honestly refuse.",
