        "title": "Deep Time: The Geologic Time Scale",
        "reading": "Earth is about 4.6 billion years old, a span so vast it is hard to imagine. Geologists organize this deep time into the geologic time scale, a calendar of Earth's history. The largest divisions are eons. We live in the Phanerozoic Eon, the age of visible life, which began 541 million years ago. Eons divide into eras, eras into periods, and periods into epochs, like chapters, sections, and paragraphs in Earth's biography.\n\nEach division is marked by major events. The Paleozoic Era saw life explode in the oceans and crawl onto land. The Mesozoic Era was the age of dinosaurs, ending when an asteroid struck 66 million years ago. The Cenozoic Era, our era, is the age of mammals. Boundaries between these chapters are often drawn at mass extinctions, when huge numbers of species vanished and new ones rose.\n\nTo grasp deep time, shrink Earth's history into one year. The planet forms on January 1. The first life appears in March. Dinosaurs arrive December 13 and vanish December 26. All of human history fits in the last minute of December 31. The geologic time scale puts our moment in perspective: we are newcomers on a very old planet, and its story dwarfs our own.",
