        "title": "The French Revolution: Liberty's Storm",
        "reading": "In 1789, France exploded into revolution. The causes had built for years: a bankrupt government, bread prices ordinary people could not afford, and a deeply unfair tax system. French society was divided into three estates. The First Estate was the clergy, the Second the nobles, and the Third Estate was everyone else, about 98 percent of the people. The Third Estate paid almost all the taxes while the other two paid almost none.\n\nInspired by Enlightenment ideas and the American example, the Third Estate demanded change. The revolution began with hope: the storming of the Bastille prison, the Declaration of the Rights of Man, and the end of feudal privileges. But the revolution soon turned on itself. Factions fought for control, and the government began executing suspected enemies by the thousands in a period called the Terror. Even revolutionary leaders ended up on the guillotine.\n\nAcross the ocean, the revolution's ideals found their truest champion in an unexpected place. In the French colony of Saint-Domingue, Toussaint Louverture, a formerly enslaved man, led the enslaved people in revolt. He applied the revolution's own words about liberty and equality to demand freedom for all, building an army that defeated European powers. While France's revolution devoured its children, Louverture used its ideals to help birth the free nation of Haiti. Ideas, once spoken, belong to everyone bold enough to claim them.",
