        "title": "Ocean Currents: Rivers in the Sea",
        "reading": "The ocean is never still. Vast rivers of water called currents flow through the seas, driven by wind, temperature, and Earth's rotation. Surface currents are powered mainly by wind. The trade winds push warm water westward near the equator, while westerlies drive currents eastward in higher latitudes. Earth's rotation bends these flows into giant circular patterns called gyres, one turning in each ocean basin.\n\nDeep currents move differently. Near the poles, cold salty water grows dense and sinks, then creeps along the ocean floor toward the equator. This slow deep circulation, called thermohaline circulation, takes about a thousand years to complete one loop. Together, surface and deep currents form a global conveyor belt that moves heat, nutrients, and oxygen around the planet.\n\nCurrents shape life in the sea. Where deep water rises to the surface in upwelling zones, it brings nutrients that feed plankton, fish, and entire food webs. Some of the world's richest fisheries sit above upwelling zones off Peru and California. Sailors have used these currents for centuries, riding them like highways across the open sea. Like trade winds that once carried revolutionary ideas across oceans, currents carry the ingredients of life to every corner of the sea.",
