        "title": "Weighing the Air",
        "reading": "If you could gather all the air in the atmosphere and weigh it, you would find about 5.5 quadrillion tons of gas. But that weight is not spread evenly. The troposphere, the lowest layer, holds roughly 75 to 80 percent of the total mass. That means three out of every four air molecules you will ever breathe are found in the bottom 7 miles of sky.\n\nThe stratosphere holds most of the rest, about 19 percent of the total. The mesosphere holds less than 1 percent, and the thermosphere and exosphere together hold only a tiny fraction of a percent. Converting these fractions to percents makes the pattern easy to see: the lower you go, the denser the air becomes.\n\nThis pattern exists because gravity pulls air molecules toward Earth. Air piles up near the surface and thins out with height, just as ocean water presses harder the deeper you dive. Pilots and mountain climbers feel the difference: at the top of Mount Everest, the air holds only about one third of the oxygen found at sea level.\n\nUnderstanding mass percents helps scientists predict weather and track pollution. When smoke or gases enter the troposphere, they stay mostly in that crowded lower layer, which is why city smog hangs near the ground instead of drifting into space.",
