        "title": "Empires Begin to Crack",
        "reading": "In 1800, Spain and Portugal ruled nearly all of Latin America, from California to the tip of South America. Colonial society was strictly ranked. At the top sat the peninsulares, Spaniards born in Spain, who held the best offices. Below them were the creoles, people of Spanish descent born in the Americas, who owned land and mines but were shut out of high government posts. Beneath them were Indigenous peoples, enslaved Africans, and people of mixed ancestry, who carried the heaviest burdens.\n\nCreoles deeply resented being treated as second-class citizens in their own homeland. When Napoleon invaded Spain in 1808 and removed the Spanish king, the empire's grip weakened. Across the Americas, creoles formed local councils called juntas and began to question why distant rulers should govern them at all. In Mexico, the priest Miguel Hidalgo issued the Grito de Dolores in 1810, calling for independence, and was soon joined by the brilliant organizer Jose Maria Morelos.\n\nThese early revolts were crushed, and both Hidalgo and Morelos were executed. But the crack in the empire kept widening. The ideas of liberty from the American, French, and Haitian revolutions had crossed the ocean, and they would not be silenced. A new generation of liberators was rising, and the largest independence movement the Americas had ever seen was about to begin.",
