        "title": "Bolivar's Vision and Guerrero's Mexico",
        "reading": "Simon Bolivar, a wealthy creole from Venezuela, became the most famous liberator of South America. After early defeats, he sought help from Alexandre Petion, the president of Haiti. Petion gave Bolivar ships, weapons, and soldiers, asking only one thing in return: that Bolivar free the enslaved people wherever his armies marched. Bolivar agreed, and his campaigns swept across Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, and Bolivia, which was named for him.\n\nIn a proclamation to his soldiers, Bolivar declared: \"We have been robbed of our freedom for three hundred years. The time has come to take back what is ours, and to build nations where all are equal before the law.\" His vision was grand: a united Latin America, free from European kings. In the end the new nations split apart, but the dream of unity inspired generations.\n\nIn Mexico, independence brought its own remarkable story. Vicente Guerrero, a general of African and Indigenous descent, rose through the ranks of the rebel army. In 1829 he became president of Mexico, one of the first Black heads of state in the Americas. One of his first acts was to abolish slavery throughout Mexico, keeping the promise of liberty that Haiti had first shown the hemisphere.",
