        "title": "The Machine Age Begins",
        "reading": "In the late 1700s, Britain became the birthplace of the Industrial Revolution, a change as deep as the invention of farming. For thousands of years, most goods had been made by hand in homes and small workshops. Then new machines, powered first by water and then by steam, let factories produce cloth, iron, and tools faster and cheaper than any hand worker could.\n\nThe steam engine, improved by James Watt in 1776, was the heart of the change. It pumped water from coal mines, drove spinning machines, and later pulled trains along iron rails. Britain had the perfect mix for industry: rich coal and iron deposits, rivers and canals for transport, money from trade, and colonies supplying raw cotton. Factories rose near the coalfields, and workers poured in from the countryside.\n\nSoon the revolution spread to Europe and America, remaking the whole world. Cities grew at a speed never seen before, railroads stitched continents together, and steamships shrank the oceans. Within a single lifetime, the world moved from muscle power to machine power, and there was no going back.\n\nThe new machines did not only change how goods were made. They changed where people lived, how long they worked, and what the air and rivers around them looked like. Progress had arrived, but it came with a price that workers would pay every day.",
