        "title": "The Scramble for Africa",
        "reading": "In 1880, European powers controlled only small strips of Africa's coastline. Within thirty years, they controlled almost the entire continent. This sudden land grab is called the Scramble for Africa. Industrial nations wanted Africa's raw materials, from rubber and palm oil to gold and diamonds, and they wanted new markets for factory goods. Rivalry added fuel: no great power wanted its neighbors to gain an advantage.\n\nIn 1884, Germany's leader Otto von Bismarck invited European powers to Berlin to set the rules for claiming African land. Fourteen nations attended, and not a single African leader was invited. The conference declared that a European claim counted only if the power actually occupied the territory, which pushed armies deeper into the continent. On paper in Berlin, Africa was divided like a pie among people who had never asked Africans what they wanted.\n\nThe borders drawn in Berlin cut straight through kingdoms, languages, and families, and many of those lines are still national borders today. Missionaries, traders, and soldiers followed the flags, and African societies were forced into colonial systems they had not chosen. The scramble was one of the fastest conquests in history, and its effects are still felt across the continent.",
