        "title": "Mapping Resources with Ratios",
        "reading": "Resource maps turn geography into numbers. A map might show that Africa holds about 30 percent of the world's known mineral reserves, or that one country produces copper at a rate of 800,000 tons per year. Ratios and rates let us compare places fairly: a ratio compares two amounts, like 3 parts cobalt to 1 part copper, while a rate compares an amount to time, like tons mined per year.\n\nPercents make continent-sized numbers easier to grasp. Saying \"30 percent of the world's mineral reserves\" is clearer than listing millions of tons. To find a percent of a continent, divide the part by the whole and multiply by 100. If 9 of Africa's 54 countries are major oil producers, that is 9 divided by 54, or about 17 percent of the continent's nations.\n\nRates tell the other half of the story: how fast resources are being used. A mine that extracts 2 million tons of ore per year will empty a 20-million-ton deposit in about ten years. When the Scramble for Africa began, Europeans raced to claim these riches. Understanding ratios, rates, and percents helps us see both the wealth of a continent and how quickly it can be spent.",
