        "title": "Unequal Treaties and a Worldwide Link",
        "reading": "The treaties China signed tell the story in the conquerors' own words. The Treaty of Nanjing of 1842 reads: \"The Emperor of China agrees to cede the island of Hong Kong to the British Crown, and to open the ports of Canton, Amoy, Foochow, Ningpo, and Shanghai to British merchants.\" Fifty-eight years later, after the Boxer Rebellion, the Boxer Protocol of 1901 reads: \"China shall pay the Powers an indemnity of 450 million taels, and foreign troops may be stationed in Peking to protect their legations.\"\n\nPlaced side by side, the two texts show a pattern: each treaty took more land, more money, or more control than the last. The Chinese people resented this deeply. In 1900, the Boxers, a movement of angry peasants and workers, rose up to drive foreigners out, but an alliance of eight foreign armies crushed them and imposed the harsh 1901 protocol.\n\nAcross the ocean, the scholar W.E.B. Du Bois was linking these struggles together. Du Bois, the great African American thinker, organized Pan-African congresses and wrote that colonialism in Asia and Africa and racism in America were branches of the same tree. He argued that colonized peoples worldwide shared one fight for dignity and self-rule, connecting China's humiliation to the struggles of Black people everywhere.",
