        "title": "Weighing Atoms",
        "reading": "Atoms are far too small to weigh on any scale, so scientists measure their mass in special units. Nearly all of an atom's mass sits in the nucleus, because protons and neutrons are each about 1,800 times heavier than an electron. For most purposes, scientists simply add the protons and neutrons: an atom with 6 protons and 6 neutrons has a mass of about 12 units.\n\nHere is the puzzle: a periodic table lists chlorine's atomic mass as about 35.5, not a whole number. The answer is that chlorine atoms come in two weights. About three out of four chlorine atoms have 18 neutrons, for a mass of 35, and about one out of four has 20 neutrons, for a mass of 37. These heavier or lighter versions of an element are called isotopes.\n\nThe listed mass of 35.5 is a weighted average: each isotope's mass counts in proportion to how common it is. Multiply 35 by 3/4 and 37 by 1/4, then add: 26.25 plus 9.25 equals 35.5. Fraction operations you already know are the key to one of science's most useful numbers, the weight of an atom.\n\nWhenever you read a periodic table, remember that most masses are averages like this. Nature mixes isotopes in fixed fractions, and the table reports the blend, not any single atom.",
