        "title": "The Periodic Table",
        "reading": "In 1869, the Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev faced a mess: more than sixty known elements with no clear order. He wrote each element on a card with its mass and properties, then arranged the cards like a game of solitaire. He placed elements in rows so that those with similar properties lined up in columns. The result was the periodic table, one of science's greatest organizing ideas.\n\nMendeleev's table was so good it predicted the future. Where his pattern had gaps, he left empty spaces and described the missing elements, including their masses and properties. Within twenty years, scientists discovered gallium and germanium, and both matched his predictions almost exactly. The table was not just a list; it was a map of undiscovered country.\n\nToday's table arranges more than 118 elements by atomic number, the count of protons, into rows called periods and columns called groups. Elements in the same group behave alike: the far-right group barely reacts with anything, while the far-left group reacts explosively with water. The table turns a hundred scattered facts into one clear pattern, and chemists use it every day.\n\nLearning the table is like learning the map of a city. Once you know the neighborhoods, you can predict what you will find there. Mendeleev's great insight was that nature loves patterns, and finding them is the heart of science.",
