        "title": "Metals, Nonmetals, and Percents",
        "reading": "Most elements are metals. Of the 118 known elements, about 94 are metals, which is roughly 80 percent. Metals share a family resemblance: they are shiny, they conduct heat and electricity well, and they can be hammered into sheets or drawn into wires. Iron, copper, gold, and aluminum are metals you meet every day.\n\nNonmetals are the smaller club, only about 18 elements, or roughly 15 percent. They include the gases you breathe, nitrogen and oxygen, plus carbon, the backbone of all living things. Nonmetals are usually dull, poor conductors, and brittle when solid. Between the two groups sit the metalloids, a handful of elements like silicon that behave a bit like both.\n\nPercents make these groups easy to compare. Saying \"80 percent metals\" instantly tells you that metals dominate the table, while \"15 percent nonmetals\" shows how special the nonmetals are. The staircase line on the periodic table marks the border between them, with metalloids touching the line. One glance at the percents tells the table's biggest story: the world of elements is mostly metal.\n\nThis matters beyond the classroom. Engineers choose metals for wires and bridges because of those shared properties, while life itself runs on nonmetals like carbon, oxygen, and nitrogen. The periodic table's percents are not trivia; they describe what the universe is mostly made of.",
