        "title": "Life in the Trenches",
        "reading": "By late 1914, the war on the Western Front had frozen into a stalemate. From the North Sea to the Swiss border stretched two lines of trenches facing each other across a blasted strip called no man's land. For four years, millions of soldiers lived in these ditches, and neither side could break through. Attacks gained a few hundred yards at the cost of thousands of lives, then the line froze again.\n\nTrench life was miserable. Rain turned the floors to deep mud that swallowed boots and sometimes men. Rats grew fat on food scraps and the bodies of the fallen. Lice tormented everyone, and standing in cold water for days caused a painful swelling called trench foot. Soldiers slept in shifts in dugouts carved into the walls, always listening for the whistle that meant an attack.\n\nBetween the lines lay no man's land, tangled with barbed wire and cratered by shells. Going over the top meant climbing out into machine-gun fire, and most attacks failed within hours. The trenches protected soldiers from bullets, but they could not protect them from boredom, fear, and the slow grinding horror of a war that would not move.\n\nMail from home, songs, and small acts of kindness kept many soldiers going. In December 1914, enemies even paused to share a Christmas truce in some sectors, a brief reminder that the men in the opposite trench were human too.",
