        "title": "The Air as a Mixture",
        "reading": "The air you are breathing is a mixture, not a compound. About 78 percent of it is nitrogen, about 21 percent is oxygen, and about 1 percent is other gases like argon and carbon dioxide. These gases are simply stirred together, not chemically joined. That is why we can separate oxygen from air by cooling it into a liquid and boiling off each gas in turn.\n\nPercent composition tells us the recipe of a mixture. To find it, divide the amount of one ingredient by the total and multiply by 100. In a 100-liter sample of air, about 78 liters are nitrogen, so nitrogen's percent composition is 78 percent. Fractions and decimals say the same thing in different clothes: 78 percent equals 78/100, which equals 0.78.\n\nThis math mattered in the trenches. Poison gas was a mixture too, and soldiers needed to know how its gases behaved to defend against them. In hospitals today, the percent composition of oxygen in breathing mixtures must be exact. Whether the mixture is air, medicine, or metal alloys, percent composition is the recipe that keeps the chemistry honest.\n\nPractice moving between the forms: 21 percent is 21/100 is 0.21. The numbers look different, but they all describe the same slice of the same air.",
