        "title": "How Atoms Hold Together",
        "reading": "Atoms join together because they are trying to become stable. Most atoms have outer shells that are not full, and a full outer shell is the most comfortable arrangement in chemistry. To get there, atoms link up with each other, and those links are called chemical bonds. Nearly everything you touch, from water to your own bones, is held together by bonds.\n\nIn an ionic bond, one atom gives electrons to another, like handing over spare change. Sodium gives one electron to chlorine, and both end up with full shells. The two atoms become charged particles called ions, and opposite charges pull them together. Table salt is the result: a crystal of sodium and chlorine ions locked in a repeating pattern.\n\nIn a covalent bond, atoms share electrons instead of trading them, like two friends sharing a bicycle. In water, oxygen shares electrons with two hydrogen atoms, and all three get the full shells they want. Sharing can be equal or unequal, but the result is a molecule: a group of atoms bonded so tightly they act as one particle.\n\nBoth kinds of bonds follow simple ratios. Table salt always joins sodium and chlorine one to one, while water always joins hydrogen and oxygen two to one. These ratios are the recipes of chemistry, and they never change for a given compound.",
