        "title": "How Democracies Fail",
        "reading": "The peace after World War I planted the seeds of the next disaster. The Treaty of Versailles punished Germany harshly, forcing it to accept blame for the war and pay huge reparations. Germans felt humiliated, Italians felt cheated of promised land, and new democracies across Europe were weak and divided. Resentment simmered through the 1920s.\n\nThen the Great Depression struck. In 1929 the American stock market crashed, and the collapse spread worldwide. Factories closed, banks failed, and millions lost their jobs and savings. Desperate people began to listen to leaders who promised order, jobs, and national greatness in exchange for their freedom. Fear became the doorway through which dictators marched.\n\nIn Italy, Benito Mussolini seized power in 1922, promising to restore Roman glory. In Germany, Adolf Hitler became chancellor in 1933, blaming Jews, communists, and the Versailles treaty for every problem. Both built totalitarian states that crushed free speech, banned rival parties, and glorified the leader. Democracies did not fall in a single day; they were dismantled piece by piece by people who promised to save them.\n\nThe lesson is sobering: democracy depends on citizens who defend it, especially in hard times. When fear is louder than facts, and when people trade freedom for promises, the door stands open for tyranny.",
