        "title": "Chemical Reactions",
        "reading": "A chemical reaction is a process that rearranges atoms into new substances. The starting materials are called reactants, and the new substances are called products. In every reaction, old bonds break and new bonds form, but no atoms are created or destroyed. Baking a cake, burning wood, and rusting iron are all chemical reactions you have seen.\n\nReactions announce themselves with clues. A color change, like an apple slice turning brown, signals new substances forming. Bubbles of gas, a release of heat or light, or a solid appearing in a clear liquid all mean a reaction is underway. Chemists read these signs the way detectives read footprints, letting observations reveal the invisible rearrangement of atoms.\n\nReactions run at different speeds. An explosion finishes in a fraction of a second, while rust can take years. Heat, concentration, and surface area all change the rate: warm reactants collide more often, and powdered solids react faster than big chunks. Understanding reactions means understanding change itself, the central story of chemistry.\n\nEvery reaction also follows the recipes you learned with bond ratios. The atoms in the reactants are the same atoms in the products, just rearranged. That is why chemists can predict exactly what a reaction will make before they ever mix a beaker.",
