        "title": "Lightning War",
        "reading": "On September 1, 1939, Germany invaded Poland, and World War II began in Europe. The attack introduced blitzkrieg, or lightning war: dive bombers shattered defenses, tanks punched through the lines, and motorized troops poured into the gaps. Poland, fighting bravely with older weapons, fell within weeks. Britain and France declared war but could do little to help.\n\nIn the spring of 1940, Germany turned west. Its armies swept through Belgium and the Netherlands, then looped around France's defenses and trapped Allied forces at Dunkirk. In a famous rescue, hundreds of small British boats crossed the Channel to carry more than 300,000 soldiers to safety. France fell in six weeks, a shock that stunned the world.\n\nBritain stood alone. Through the summer and fall of 1940, German bombers pounded London and other cities in the Battle of Britain, but British pilots and radar held the line. In June 1941, Hitler broke his pact with the Soviet Union and invaded with three million men, opening the largest land war in history. The lightning war had conquered much of Europe, but its greatest tests were still ahead.\n\nBlitzkrieg worked by speed and shock, but speed has limits. Supply lines stretched thin, winters froze engines, and conquered peoples began to resist. The fast victories of 1939 and 1940 would give way to a long, grinding war.",
