        "title": "The Pacific War",
        "reading": "On the morning of December 7, 1941, Japanese planes attacked the American naval base at Pearl Harbor, Hawaii, without warning. More than 2,400 Americans were killed, and much of the Pacific fleet was damaged. President Roosevelt called it \"a date which will live in infamy,\" and the United States entered World War II. Japan had already seized territory across Asia, and now it struck across the Pacific.\n\nAmerica answered with a strategy called island-hopping. Instead of attacking every Japanese-held island, American forces captured key islands and skipped the heavily defended ones, cutting them off from supplies. Marines fought brutal battles on Guadalcanal, Iwo Jima, and Okinawa, moving steadily closer to Japan. Japanese pilots flew suicide missions called kamikaze attacks, a sign of how desperate the fighting had become.\n\nIn August 1945, the United States dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, weapons of terrible new power. Japan surrendered on September 2, 1945, ending the war. The Pacific war had lasted nearly four years and cost millions of lives, and its end forced the world to face the terrible power of the atomic age.\n\nAmid the destruction, individual courage shone. Doris Miller, a Black cook aboard the battleship West Virginia at Pearl Harbor, carried wounded sailors to safety and manned an anti-aircraft gun he had never been trained to use. He became one of the first heroes of the war and received the Navy Cross.",
