        "title": "Two Home Fronts",
        "reading": "While soldiers fought overseas, the war changed life at home in very different ways for different Americans. For most families, the home front meant sacrifice and work: food and gasoline were rationed, women took factory jobs as Rosie the Riveter became a national symbol, and children collected scrap metal and bought war bonds. A factory worker wrote: \"We worked ten-hour shifts and were proud of it. Every plane we built meant a boy coming home sooner.\"\n\nFor Japanese Americans, the home front meant injustice. After Pearl Harbor, fear and prejudice led the government to order more than 120,000 people of Japanese descent, most of them American citizens, into internment camps behind barbed wire. Families lost homes, farms, and businesses. A teenager in a camp wrote: \"We pledge allegiance to the same flag every morning, behind the same barbed wire. I wonder when America will see us as Americans.\"\n\nPlaced side by side, the two accounts ask a hard question: what does loyalty mean when a country fears its own people? Many young Japanese American men answered by volunteering, and the 442nd Regimental Combat Team became one of the most decorated units in American history. Their service, like Doris Miller's at Pearl Harbor, proved that courage and patriotism belong to all Americans.",
