        "title": "Matter Is Never Lost",
        "reading": "In the 1700s, the French chemist Antoine Lavoisier discovered one of science's deepest rules: in a chemical reaction, mass is never lost. This rule is called the conservation of mass. It means that the total mass of everything you start with always equals the total mass of everything you end with, no matter how dramatic the change looks.\n\nThe rule is easy to miss. Burn a log and the ashes weigh far less than the log did, which seems to break the rule. But the missing mass did not vanish: it escaped as gases like carbon dioxide and water vapor, plus heat and light. If you burned the log inside a sealed container and weighed everything, gases included, the total would match the original log exactly.\n\nThe reason is simple: reactions only rearrange atoms. No atoms are created and none are destroyed, so the total mass cannot change. Lavoisier proved this by weighing sealed flasks before and after reactions, showing that careful measurement always balances. His rule became the foundation of modern chemistry.\n\nEvery balanced chemical equation is the conservation of mass in action. The numbers in front of each formula make sure the atoms, and therefore the mass, match on both sides. Input equals output: that is the whole rule in three words.",
