        "title": "Remembering the Holocaust",
        "reading": "Between 1933 and 1945, the Nazi government of Germany carried out the Holocaust, the deliberate murder of six million Jewish people. It began with laws, not violence: the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 stripped Jews of citizenship and banned marriages between Jews and non-Jews. Jewish businesses were seized, children were expelled from schools, and families were forced into crowded ghettos.\n\nThe persecution grew into genocide. Jews from across occupied Europe were deported to camps, where most were murdered. Six million Jews died, two thirds of Europe's Jewish population. The Nazis also murdered Roma people, disabled people, political prisoners, and others they declared enemies. The scale of the killing is almost impossible to grasp, which is why we remember it in individual stories, not only in numbers.\n\nEven in the darkest places, people resisted. In 1943, the Jews of the Warsaw Ghetto rose up against the German army and fought for weeks. Across Europe, rescuers hid Jewish families at the risk of their own lives. After the war, survivors bore witness so the world would know. Their courage reminds us that dignity can survive even when everything else is taken.\n\nRemembrance is a promise. Memorials, museums, and days of remembrance around the world keep the victims' names alive and teach each new generation. To remember is to refuse to let hatred win twice.",
