        "title": "Measuring Intervals",
        "reading": "History runs on intervals, the gaps between dates. The Nazi regime lasted from 1933 to 1945: subtract, and the interval is 12 years. World War II in Europe ran from 1939 to 1945, an interval of 6 years. Computing intervals is simple subtraction, but it turns a list of dates into a sense of how long people endured events.\n\nSpeed problems work the same careful way. Start with speed equals distance over time, then rearrange it. To find distance, multiply speed by time: a train moving at 80 kilometers per hour for 3 hours covers 240 kilometers. To find time, divide distance by speed: a 240-kilometer trip at 80 kilometers per hour takes 3 hours.\n\nIntervals and speeds both reward careful bookkeeping. Write down what you know, pick the right form of the relationship, and compute step by step. A timeline interval is a distance in time, and the same honest math measures both journeys through space and journeys through history.\n\nPractice with real dates: the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising began in 1943, two years before the war in Europe ended in 1945. The Nuremberg Laws came in 1935, ten years before the regime fell. Each interval is a small act of remembrance, measuring how long history's chapters lasted.",
