        "title": "Two Superpowers",
        "reading": "When World War II ended in 1945, the world had two superpowers left standing: the United States and the Soviet Union. They had fought together against Hitler, but they trusted each other little and wanted very different futures. America championed capitalism and democracy, while the Soviet Union spread communism and one-party rule. The stage was set for a new kind of conflict.\n\nWinston Churchill gave it a name in 1946, declaring that an \"iron curtain\" had fallen across Europe. Behind it, the Soviets controlled Eastern Europe through allied governments and, in 1955, the Warsaw Pact military alliance. In the West, America and its partners formed NATO in 1949, promising that an attack on one would be treated as an attack on all. Europe was divided into two armed camps.\n\nBoth sides soon held nuclear weapons, and deterrence became the grim logic of the age: neither dared attack because both could be destroyed in return. The superpowers avoided fighting each other directly and competed instead through proxy wars, spying, and a race to build ever more terrible weapons. It was a standoff that would last more than forty years.\n\nFor ordinary people, the Cold War meant drills, headlines, and fear. Schoolchildren practiced ducking under desks, and families built backyard shelters. The long standoff shaped daily life for two generations.",
