        "title": "Speeding Up",
        "reading": "Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes. If a car goes from 0 to 60 kilometers per hour in 6 seconds, it is accelerating. The unit is meters per second squared, written m/s2, which really means how many meters per second of speed are gained each second. Speed tells you how fast you move; acceleration tells you how fast your speed is changing.\n\nAcceleration has a direction too. Pressing the gas pedal is positive acceleration, while braking is negative acceleration, sometimes called deceleration. Turning the steering wheel also counts, because changing direction changes velocity even if speed stays the same. Anytime velocity changes in size or direction, acceleration is happening.\n\nFalling objects show acceleration at work. Near Earth, gravity accelerates everything downward at about 9.8 m/s2, which means a falling rock gains about 9.8 meters per second of speed every second. Air resistance eventually limits the fall, but without air, a feather and a hammer would hit the ground together, as astronauts once showed on the Moon.\n\nThe arms race accelerated too, in the mathematical sense: weapons stockpiles did not just grow, they grew faster every decade. When growth itself speeds up, you are seeing acceleration, whether in physics or in history.",
