        "title": "Graphing the Arms Race",
        "reading": "Numbers tell the Cold War's scariest story. In 1950, the two superpowers held only hundreds of nuclear weapons combined. By the 1980s, they held tens of thousands. The growth was not steady; it accelerated, with each decade adding more weapons than the last. Graphing the numbers turns this frightening pattern into a curve you can see.\n\nPercent change measures each leap. The formula is the same one you used for carbon dioxide: subtract the old number from the new number, divide by the old number, and multiply by 100. If warheads grow from 1,000 to 10,000, the change is 9,000 divided by 1,000, or 900 percent. Percent change shows that early growth, when numbers are small, can be the most explosive in relative terms.\n\nTo graph the growth, put years on the horizontal axis and weapons on the vertical axis, then plot each point. The Cold War curve bends upward steeply, the signature shape of acceleration. Reading such graphs is a life skill: the same upward-bending curve appears in populations, prices, and pandemics. The math is neutral; what matters is what we choose to do with the warning.\n\nTry it yourself: a stockpile grows from 500 to 2,000 weapons. The percent change is 1,500 divided by 500, or 300 percent. One formula, honestly applied, reveals how fast any race is running.",
