        "title": "Ghana Leads the Way",
        "reading": "For almost a hundred years, the land we now call Ghana was a British colony known as the Gold Coast. British companies controlled its gold, cocoa, and busy ports, while the African people who lived there had almost no say in how their country was run. After World War II, African soldiers came home and asked a hard question: why had they fought for freedom abroad but had none at home.\n\nOne of the loudest voices asking that question was Kwame Nkrumah, a teacher who had studied in the United States and Britain. He believed Africans should govern themselves, so he built a political party of workers, farmers, and students. When the British jailed him in 1950, his party won the election anyway, and he was released to become prime minister.\n\nOn March 6, 1957, Nkrumah stood before a huge crowd in Accra and declared Ghana independent. He told them: \"At long last, the battle has ended. Ghana, your beloved country, is free forever. We must learn to live the strong and free life of the African people.\"\n\nGhana was the first country south of the Sahara to win independence, and its black star flag became a symbol of hope across the continent. Nkrumah dreamed of a united Africa, an idea called pan-Africanism, and his victory helped spark freedom movements from Kenya to Nigeria in the years that followed.",
