        "title": "Freedom's Wave",
        "reading": "Ghana's freedom in 1957 was only the beginning. Over the next decade, colony after colony won independence, and 1960 became known as the Year of Africa because seventeen new nations joined the United Nations. In Kenya, Jomo Kenyatta led the fight against British rule. In Algeria, a long and bitter war finally ended French control in 1962.\n\nNot every path was peaceful. In the Congo, independence leader Patrice Lumumba was arrested and killed within months of freedom, and the country fell into chaos. Portugal refused to let go of its colonies at all, so freedom fighters in Angola and Mozambique waged long guerrilla wars through the 1960s and 1970s.\n\nNew nations faced hard questions right away. Colonial rulers had drawn borders without asking the people who lived there, so new countries held many different peoples inside straight lines on a map. Leaders had to build schools, roads, and governments almost from nothing, while older powers still wanted their oil, gold, and cocoa.\n\nStill, the wave changed the world. African nations now voted in the United Nations and spoke with one voice on big questions. As Nkrumah had hoped, the freedom of Ghana lit a path that millions of others followed, and the map of Africa was redrawn by Africans themselves.",
