        "title": "Newton's First Law: Inertia",
        "reading": "Sir Isaac Newton was an English scientist who, in 1687, wrote three simple rules that describe how everything moves, from a rolling ball to the planets. His first law is sometimes called the law of inertia. It says that an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion keeps moving at the same speed and in the same direction, unless a force acts on it.\n\nInertia is the tendency of matter to resist changes in its motion. That is why your body leans forward when a bus stops suddenly: your body wants to keep moving even though the bus has stopped. It is also why a hockey puck slides so far on smooth ice, where very little friction fights against it.\n\nYou feel the first law every day. When a car turns a corner, you seem to slide toward the outside of the turn, but really your body is just trying to keep going straight. Seat belts and air bags exist because engineers know your body will obey Newton's first law in a crash.\n\nThe first law also explains motion in space. A spacecraft far from any planet will drift forever in a straight line at a steady speed, with no fuel needed, because there is almost no friction in space to slow it down. Motion does not need a push to continue. It only needs a push to change.",
