        "reading": "In 1947, Britain gave up its largest colony: India. The independence movement had been led for decades by Mohandas Gandhi, who taught nonviolent resistance, and by millions of ordinary Indians who refused to obey unjust laws. But independence came with a painful price. British India was split, or partitioned, into two nations: mostly Hindu India and mostly Muslim Pakistan.\n\nThe partition forced about fifteen million people to move, as Hindus and Sikhs fled to India and Muslims fled to Pakistan. Terrible violence broke out along the way, and hundreds of thousands of people died. Two nations were born, but both carried deep wounds from the division.\n\nOn the night of independence, India's leader Jawaharlal Nehru spoke to his people: \"At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom.\" His words promised hope and a fresh start.\n\nAcross the new border, Pakistan's leader Muhammad Ali Jinnah spoke of a different dream: \"You are free to go to your temples. You are free to go to your mosques. You may belong to any religion, but you are all citizens of Pakistan.\" Both speeches promised freedom, but each new nation would have to work for generations to keep those promises.",
