        "title": "The Canal and the New Lines",
        "reading": "While Asia's colonies won freedom, the Middle East faced its own upheaval. After World War I, the Ottoman Empire collapsed, and Britain and France drew new borders across the region almost as they pleased. They called these areas mandates, but to the Arab peoples living there, it felt like a new kind of colonial rule.\n\nIn 1948, the new state of Israel was created in the midst of Arab lands, and war broke out at once. Hundreds of thousands of Palestinian Arabs fled or were forced from their homes. The lines drawn on maps by outsiders became battle lines, and the conflict has echoed through the decades.\n\nThen in 1956, Egypt's president Gamal Abdel Nasser made a bold move: he nationalized the Suez Canal, taking control of the waterway from Britain and France. Ships passing between Europe and Asia paid tolls to Egypt now, not to European companies. Britain, France, and Israel attacked Egypt, but world pressure forced them to back down.\n\nNasser became a hero across the Arab world, a symbol of standing up to old colonial powers. Oil made the region even more important to the world, and the new nations of the Middle East, drawn along old colonial lines, began shaping their own futures.",
