        "title": "The Montgomery Boycott",
        "reading": "On December 1, 1955, Rosa Parks, a Black seamstress in Montgomery, Alabama, refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger. She was arrested, and her quiet courage lit a spark. Black leaders in Montgomery called for a boycott of the city buses, and a young minister named Martin Luther King Jr. was chosen to lead it.\n\nThe boycott lasted 381 days. Black residents walked to work, shared carpools, and even rode mules rather than ride segregated buses. They faced threats, arrests, and the bombing of King's home, but they answered with nonviolence. They refused to fight back, even when attacked.\n\nThe strategy worked. The bus company lost money every day the boycott continued, and in 1956 the Supreme Court ruled that segregated buses were unconstitutional. The boycotters had won, and they had shown the nation a new way to fight injustice.\n\nNews of Montgomery spread around the world. In South Africa and India, people fighting their own battles against unfair rule studied the boycott's methods. A local protest about bus seats had become a lesson in courage for freedom movements everywhere.\n\nThe boycott also built new leaders. King was only 26 when it began, and the movement's success made him famous across America. Ordinary people learned they had power too: maids, janitors, and students who walked miles each day discovered that courage is contagious. Montgomery proved that a whole community, acting together, could bend history.",
