        "title": "A War in the Jungle",
        "reading": "After World War II, the Southeast Asian nation of Vietnam was split in two: a communist north and a non-communist south. The United States feared that if one country fell to communism, its neighbors would fall too, like a row of dominoes. So America sent money, then advisers, and then hundreds of thousands of soldiers to help South Vietnam.\n\nThe war was unlike any America had fought. The enemy, the Viet Cong, knew the jungles and used tunnels, traps, and surprise attacks. American soldiers, many of them teenagers drafted straight out of high school, fought in thick heat and rain against an enemy they could rarely see.\n\nAs the years dragged on, victory stayed out of reach. The war cost a fortune and filled the nightly news with terrible images. By 1968, after the surprise Tet Offensive showed the enemy was far from beaten, many Americans began to ask what the war was really for.\n\nThe United States finally withdrew in 1973, and in 1975 North Vietnam took over the south. More than 58,000 Americans and millions of Vietnamese had died. The war left deep scars on everyone it touched, and Americans are still debating its lessons today.\n\nThe war's cost went beyond battlefields. Agent Orange, a chemical sprayed to clear jungle, poisoned soldiers and villagers alike. Back home, veterans returned to a country unsure how to welcome them. Remembering Vietnam means honoring their service while learning its hard lessons.",
