        "reading": "For more than forty years, the Soviet Union and the United States faced off in the Cold War. The Soviets controlled Eastern Europe with an iron grip, and the Berlin Wall, built in 1961, kept East Germans locked inside the communist half of their divided city. To the world, the Soviet empire looked permanent and unbreakable.\n\nBut by the 1980s the system was rotting from within. Store shelves stood empty, factories made shoddy goods, and ordinary people lost faith. In 1985, a new leader, Mikhail Gorbachev, tried to save the system with two bold policies: glasnost, or openness, which let people criticize the government, and perestroika, or restructuring, which loosened state control of the economy.\n\nInstead of saving the empire, openness exposed how broken it was. Newspapers printed the truth about past crimes and present failures. People in Poland, Hungary, and Czechoslovakia demanded the right to choose their own governments.\n\nIn 1987, President Ronald Reagan stood in West Berlin and challenged Gorbachev directly: \"Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall!\" Two years later, crowds did exactly that, smashing the wall with hammers as the world watched in amazement.\n\nGlasnost also freed the press to report on disasters the government once hid, like the Chernobyl nuclear accident. Citizens who had whispered complaints in kitchens began speaking openly in squares. The empire's leaders had hoped openness would save them. Instead it gave people the truth, and the truth set them free.",
