        "title": "The End of an Empire",
        "reading": "The fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989 set off a chain reaction. One by one, the nations of Eastern Europe threw off communist rule: Poland, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Romania, and others chose their own leaders in free elections. The empire that had seemed permanent was crumbling in months.\n\nThen the Soviet Union itself broke apart. Its fifteen republics, from Ukraine to Kazakhstan, declared independence. On Christmas Day 1991, the Soviet flag came down over the Kremlin for the last time. The Cold War was over, and Russia stood alone where a superpower had been.\n\nAs the Cold War ended, another long struggle reached its turning point. In South Africa, Nelson Mandela walked free in 1990 after 27 years in prison for fighting apartheid. With the world's attention no longer split by the Cold War, pressure grew on South Africa to end white minority rule, and Mandela would become president in 1994.\n\nThe map of the world was redrawn once again. New nations joined the United Nations, old enemies tried to become partners, and people everywhere wondered what kind of world would rise from the empire's ashes.\n\nThe new nations faced enormous challenges: building economies, writing constitutions, and settling old scores. Some, like Poland and the Czech Republic, built stable democracies. Others struggled with corruption and conflict. Freedom, the world learned again, is only the beginning of the story.",
