        "title": "Lines on a Map",
        "reading": "After World War I, Britain and France carved up the fallen Ottoman Empire and drew the modern Middle East almost as they pleased. They created mandates in Palestine, Syria, Lebanon, and Iraq, ruling these lands while promising eventual independence. The borders they drew cut through peoples and tribes, planting conflicts that still burn today.\n\nIn 1948, the British mandate in Palestine ended, and the state of Israel was born. War broke out immediately with neighboring Arab states. Hundreds of thousands of Palestinian Arabs fled or were forced from their homes, becoming refugees. The question of who rightfully owns that land has divided the region ever since.\n\nOil changed everything. Vast petroleum reserves lay under the deserts of Saudi Arabia, Iraq, Kuwait, and Iran. The world ran on oil, so these new nations suddenly held enormous power. Whoever controlled the oil could shape the world's economy.\n\nThe lines on the map, drawn by outsiders, kept causing trouble. Kurds found themselves split across four countries. Sunnis and Shias, two branches of Islam, competed for power inside new borders. The modern Middle East was born from these lines, and its people have been redrawing their future ever since.\n\nThe Kurds, numbering in the tens of millions, remain the world's largest people without their own country, split across Turkey, Iraq, Syria, and Iran. Their story shows how arbitrary lines can wound for a century. Every border in the region carries a history, and understanding that history is the first step toward peace.",
