        "title": "Why Governments Exist",
        "reading": "Imagine a town with no rules, no police, and no one to fix the roads. Who would stop a thief? Who would build a bridge? Life would be unsafe and chaotic. Governments exist to solve these problems: they keep order, protect people, and provide services no one person could manage alone.\n\nThere are many kinds of government. In a monarchy, a king or queen rules, sometimes sharing power with elected leaders. In a dictatorship, one person or small group holds all power and punishes anyone who disagrees. In a democracy, the people themselves hold power and choose their leaders by voting.\n\nThe United States is a republic, a kind of democracy where citizens elect representatives to make laws for them. It is also federal, meaning power is shared between the national government in Washington and the fifty state governments. Neither level can boss the other around completely.\n\nGovernments can only do their job if citizens take part. Voting, obeying just laws, paying taxes, and speaking up about problems are all part of the deal. A government is only as good as the people who watch over it.\n\nEven the smallest governments follow this logic. Your school has rules, hall monitors, and a principal, a tiny government keeping order. Your family has rules too, with parents as the executive branch. From families to nations, people create governments for the same reason: life together needs agreed rules.",
