        "title": "Sound: Waves in the Air",
        "reading": "Sound is a wave that travels through matter by squeezing and stretching it. When a drum is struck, its skin pushes nearby air molecules together, then pulls back, creating a chain of compressions and rarefactions that races outward. Your eardrum vibrates when those waves arrive, and your brain reads the vibrations as sound.\n\nSound needs a material to travel through, which is why there is no sound in the empty vacuum of space. In air, sound moves at about 343 meters per second. It moves faster through water and fastest through solid steel, because tightly packed molecules pass vibrations along more quickly.\n\nPitch comes from frequency. A high-pitched whistle vibrates hundreds of times per second, while a low tuba note vibrates far fewer times. Loudness comes from amplitude: bigger vibrations push your eardrum harder, and your brain hears a louder sound.\n\nEvery sound you hear is a wave story. A guitar string's vibration becomes waves in air, which become vibrations in your ear, which become signals in your brain. From pluck to perception, it is waves all the way down.\n\nTry feeling sound. Hum a low note and touch your throat: you will feel the vibrations that become waves in the air. Hum higher, and the vibrations quicken. You are a sound source, your throat a living speaker, turning muscle motion into waves your friends' ears can decode.",
