        "title": "A Living Document",
        "reading": "The founders knew their work was imperfect, so they built in a way to change it: the amendment process. The first ten amendments, the Bill of Rights, were added in 1791, protecting freedoms of speech, religion, and the press. The Constitution could grow with the nation.\n\nIts greatest growth came after the Civil War. The 13th Amendment ended slavery. The 14th Amendment declared that all persons born in the United States are citizens and promised every person equal protection of the laws. The 15th Amendment said the right to vote could not be denied because of race. These three amendments rewrote the Constitution's promises to include Black Americans.\n\nOne founder explained why power must be divided in Federalist 51: \"If men were angels, no government would be necessary. If angels were to govern men, no controls on government would be necessary. In framing a government which is to be administered by men over men, you must first enable the government to control the governed, and then oblige it to control itself.\"\n\nThe Constitution has been amended 27 times, and each amendment tells a story of Americans demanding that the nation's promises match its principles. It is called a living document because it keeps growing.",
