        "title": "Circuits: Paths for Electricity",
        "reading": "Electricity becomes useful when it flows in a loop called a circuit. A circuit needs three parts: a power source like a battery, a path for the current to travel, usually wires, and something that uses the energy, like a light bulb. Break the loop anywhere, and the current stops everywhere.\n\nCurrent is the flow of tiny charged particles called electrons, measured in amperes, or amps. Voltage, measured in volts, is the push that drives the current, like water pressure in a pipe. Resistance, measured in ohms, is whatever fights the flow, like a narrow spot in the pipe.\n\nA simple circuit is easy to build: connect a battery to a bulb with two wires, and the bulb glows. Add a switch, and you can open and close the loop with a flick. Every flashlight you have ever used is a circuit with a battery, a bulb, and a switch.\n\nUnderstanding circuits starts with seeing the loop. Engineers draw circuit diagrams with symbols for each part, mapping the path current will follow. Once you can read the map, you can build anything from a doorbell to a robot.\n\nYou can build a circuit right now. A battery, two wires, and a small bulb from a flashlight are all you need. Touch one wire to each battery terminal and each side of the bulb's base, and it glows. You have just joined every electrician in history: power source, path, and load.",
