        "title": "Three Branches, Shared Power",
        "reading": "The founders feared concentrated power, so they split the federal government into three branches. The legislative branch, Congress, makes the laws. The executive branch, led by the president, carries the laws out. The judicial branch, the courts, interprets the laws and settles disputes.\n\nEach branch can check the others, a system called checks and balances. Congress passes a bill, but the president can veto it, sending it back. Congress can override that veto with a two-thirds vote. The president appoints judges, but the Senate must approve them, and courts can strike down laws that violate the Constitution.\n\nThe system is slow on purpose. The founders wanted big decisions to require agreement among many people, not just one ruler's order. A bill must survive debate in two houses of Congress, the president's desk, and possibly a court's review.\n\nChecks and balances do not run on their own. They work because citizens vote, because journalists report, and because ordinary people demand that each branch stay in its lane. The machinery of government needs the people to keep it honest.\n\nState governments copy the same design. Each state has a governor, a legislature, and courts, with their own checks and balances. Your city likely has a mayor, a council, and local judges. The founders' pattern repeats at every level, so power stays divided close to home too.",
