        "title": "Magnets: Invisible Push and Pull",
        "reading": "Magnets pull and push without ever touching, thanks to an invisible magnetic field around them. Every magnet has two poles, north and south. Opposite poles attract, and like poles repel, which is why two magnets snap together one way and fight each other when flipped.\n\nEarth itself is a giant magnet. Its magnetic field stretches far into space and shields us from harmful solar wind. A compass needle is simply a tiny magnet lining up with Earth's field, pointing north wherever you stand.\n\nMagnets are sorted by strength. A fridge magnet barely holds a photo, while a neodymium magnet the size of a coin can lift many pounds. The strength depends on the material and how its atoms line up: in a strong magnet, countless atomic magnets all point the same way.\n\nYou use magnets every day without noticing. Speakers turn electrical signals into sound with magnets, credit cards store data on magnetic strips, and refrigerator doors seal with magnetic strips. The invisible push and pull is everywhere.\n\nTry mapping a field. Sprinkle iron filings on paper over a magnet and tap gently: the filings line up along invisible field lines, drawing the magnet's hidden shape. You are seeing the invisible made visible. Scientists use the same trick, with finer tools, to map Earth's field from space.",
