        "title": "Electromagnets: Magnets You Can Switch",
        "reading": "An electromagnet is a magnet you can turn on and off. Wrap wire around an iron nail, connect the wire to a battery, and the nail becomes a magnet as long as current flows. Disconnect the battery, and the magnetism vanishes.\n\nThe secret is that electric current creates a magnetic field. Each loop of wire adds its field to the others, so more loops mean a stronger magnet. More current means a stronger magnet too. Engineers control both to dial the strength up or down.\n\nElectromagnets do heavy work. Scrap yards use giant electromagnets to lift whole cars: switch on to grab, switch off to drop. Maglev trains float above their tracks on powerful electromagnets, gliding with no friction at all.\n\nThey also do delicate work. Doorbells, speakers, and hard drives all use small electromagnets. The same principle that lifts a car also writes your files to disk. Control the current, and you control the magnet.\n\nYou can build one today. Wrap insulated wire fifty times around a nail, connect the ends to a battery, and test what it lifts. Add more wraps and test again: the lifting power grows. You are controlling magnetism with electricity, exactly as engineers do in scrap yards and hospitals.",
