        "title": "Good Trouble",
        "reading": "Sometimes making a law takes more than procedure: it takes courage. John Lewis was a teenager when he joined the civil rights movement, and in 1965 he led marchers across the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma, where troopers beat him bloody. He never stopped believing that ordinary people could change the law.\n\nLewis later carried that fight into Congress, serving Georgia in the House of Representatives for 33 years. He called his approach \"good trouble\": peaceful, principled disruption that forces the nation to face injustice. He staged sit-ins on the House floor to demand votes on gun laws.\n\nLewis helped pass and protect the great civil rights laws, including the Voting Rights Act. He knew that laws are not just words on paper; they are promises that must be defended. When later Congresses tried to weaken voting protections, Lewis fought to save them.\n\nHis life shows the full journey of a law. It begins with people in the streets, moves through marches and speeches, survives the obstacle course of Congress, and lands on the president's desk. Then citizens like Lewis spend their lives making sure the law keeps its promise.\n\nLewis's colleagues called him the conscience of Congress. When he spoke, even opponents listened, because his moral authority came from lived sacrifice. He was arrested more than 40 times in his life, yet never with bitterness. His example teaches that courage and kindness can share the same heart.",
