        "title": "How America Elects a President",
        "reading": "Every four years, Americans choose a president through a system the founders invented: the Electoral College. When you vote, you are really voting for a slate of electors pledged to your candidate. Each state gets electors equal to its senators plus representatives, for 538 total.\n\nTo win, a candidate needs 270 electoral votes, a majority. Most states award all their electors to the statewide winner, so winning California by one vote or one million votes earns the same 55 electors. That is why candidates campaign hardest in closely divided swing states.\n\nThis system can surprise people. Five times in history, the winner of the popular vote lost the election, most recently in 2000 and 2016. Supporters say it forces candidates to care about small states; critics say every vote should count equally.\n\nWhatever you think of the system, the ballot is where citizens speak. Local elections for mayor, sheriff, and school board often matter more for daily life than the presidency, and they are frequently decided by a handful of votes. Showing up is what counts.\n\nThe system has sparked many reform ideas. Some propose awarding electors by congressional district; others want a national popular vote. Amending the Constitution is deliberately hard, so the Electoral College endures. Debating it, though, is itself good citizenship: questioning the rules is how republics stay healthy.",
